[b]Kitsunebi[/b], a night glass interface for 7 Days to Die V3.x: deep indigo glass panels, fox-fire cyan accents and gold titles, across every screen in the game.

The theme is XML and images only. It changes how the game looks, never how it plays, and it runs with EAC on.

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]About the screenshots[/color][/b]

The screenshots were taken with [b]separate[/b] inventory and container mods installed (a bigger backpack, extra crafting queue and workstation slots, bigger stacks), to show the theme stretching to fit them. [b][u]Those mods are not included in KitsuneUI. With KitsuneUI alone, your bags and workstations keep their vanilla sizes.[/u][/b]

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]What it covers[/color][/b]

[list]
[*]  [b]HUD:[/b] health, stamina, food and water bars with an injury hatch, buff pop-outs lined up with the bars, compass, location box, quest and recipe trackers, party bars, enemy health bar
[*]  [b]Toolbelt:[/b] a glass tray that grows to fit one or two bars, or one bar of up to 20 slots, with each slot's key number in its corner
[*]  [b]Inventory:[/b] backpack, loot, vehicle and drone storage, with rounded slots that glow fox-fire when selected and gold on hover
[*]  [b]Crafting:[/b] recipe list, recipe and item details, crafting queue, the creative menu, every workstation
[*]  [b]Trader and quests:[/b] trader stock, services, job offer, quest journal, turn-in rewards
[*]  [b]Character:[/b] character sheet, buffs, skills and perks (skills you can study bright, locked ones dim; with KitsuneUI_Settings, fully learned ones gold), challenges, cosmetics
[*]  [b]Map:[/b] map, waypoints, shared waypoints and the right-click menus
[*]  [b]Menus:[/b] the news screen, the main menu as a glass rail down the left edge (16:9 and ultrawide), Play Game with your saves as cards (with each world's map, with KitsuneUI_Settings), the server browser, the in-game Esc menu, every Options page, death message and respawn screen
[*]  [b]Loading screen:[/b] the picture fills the screen and the tip sits in a glass column down the right edge, with the spawn buttons at its foot
[*]  [b]Everything else:[/b] chat, the player list, power and trap windows, cameras, signs and the sign editor, keypads, tooltips, context menus, tips, message boxes, admin and debug panels
[/list]
[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]Optional add-ons[/color][/b]

Five small extras, each in its own zip. Settings, Toolbelt20, JobPreview and Practice contain a DLL, so they need [b]EAC off[/b]. Loading is XML and images only.

[list]
[*]  [color=rgb(217, 70, 239)][b]KitsuneUI_Settings[/b][/color][b]:[/b] an in-game settings window ([b]Esc menu > KitsuneUI[/b]) with eight pages of options on a rail down the left, Move HUD and layout profiles. See [b]The Settings add-on[/b] below. Needs KitsuneUI.
[*]  [color=rgb(217, 70, 239)][b]KitsuneUI_Loading[/b][/color][b]:[/b] six Kitsunebi night paintings for the loading screen in place of the vanilla pictures (a hilltop den, a ghost town, a burnt forest, a desert gas station, a frozen radio tower and horde night), plus four KitsuneUI loading tips. With KitsuneUI_Settings the pictures take turns, so you see every one. On a server, install it on the [b]server and every player's game[/b].
[*]  [color=rgb(217, 70, 239)][b]KitsuneUI_Practice[/b][/color][b]:[/b] for [url=https://github.com/Kitsune-Den/KitsuneShugyo]Kitsune Shugyō[/url]: a Practice tab with a bar for every attribute and crafting skill (hover a row for where that practice came from), and practice bars instead of Buy buttons on the attribute screen. Needs KitsuneUI; does nothing without Shugyō. On a server, install it on the server and every player's game.
[*]  [color=rgb(217, 70, 239)][b]KitsuneUI_Toolbelt20[/b][/color][b]:[/b] two toolbelt bars of 10, or one bar of 11 to 20 slots. With two bars, number keys use the bottom bar and shift + number the top bar (marked with a SHIFT tag, and its slot numbers are gold); with one bar, slots past 10 are shift + 1, 2, ... The server sets the size, or the Settings add-on in single player and games you host. Turns on the toolbelt size setting the game already has but only reads in the prefab editor. Works with or without KitsuneUI.
[*]  [color=rgb(217, 70, 239)][b]KitsuneUI_JobPreview[/b][/color][b]:[/b] trader jobs as picture tiles under the trader, with each location's preview image, name, tier and distance, and a job offer window with the location's picture, distance, biome and objectives. With KitsuneUI_Settings it can be switched back to the game's dialog list. Needs KitsuneUI.
[/list]
[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]The Settings add-on[/color][/b]

Esc menu > KitsuneUI opens a settings window with eight pages on a rail down the left:

[list]
[*]  [b]HUD:[/b] toolbelt slot numbers, enemy health bar always shown, fade the toolbelt and stat bars when idle, a horde night countdown under the day and time, crafting progress on the HUD, an action bar while you reload, eat, drink or use medicine, time left beside buffs, and a warning ring on toolbelt items that are nearly worn out, and with Toolbelt20 the toolbelt size (two bars of 10, stacked or side by side, or one bar of 10 to 20)
[*]  [b]Map:[/b] a minimap drawn from your explored map, with the map's markers (quest targets, waypoints, bed, backpack, vehicles, party), zoom (64, 128 or 256 blocks), north up or turn with me (following the vehicle you drive too), names for players and waypoints, a square or round map, your coordinates and the day and time under it, and creatures as dots (animals, or animals and zombies)
[*]  [b]Combat:[/b] ElvUI-style unit frames by the crosshair (your frame and your target's, with buff and debuff icons above them), party frames under yours, nameplates over enemies you're fighting, floating combat text, frame width, buff icon size and how many buffs to show, debuffs in their own row with a countdown sweep on every icon, and portraits (a real picture of every zombie, trader and bandit, painted ones for animals, and a snapshot of your own character)
[*]  [b]Windows:[/b] show the whole backpack without scrolling, a search box in the backpack that dims everything that doesn't match (V3.3; V3.4 has its own filters), trader jobs as picture tiles or the game's normal dialog list (at 100 to 150%), backpack width (Normal, Wide, Extra wide) with a whole-backpack button in the bag, text size (90 to 130%), quality rings on item slots, compare with the item in your hand, and quest tracker progress bars and a direction arrow, and trader jobs by tier, by distance or with no tiers (nearest first, no difficulty skulls: scout the danger yourself)
[*]  [b]Look:[/b] glass strength (100, 85, 70 or 55%), a glass colour (Midnight, Charcoal, Obsidian, Forest, Plum, Ember, Ocean), colour presets (Kitsunebi, Ember, Moss, Sakura) or a custom colour from 12 hues, bar text (100/100, 100%, value only or none), a separate XP bar above the toolbelt, and 15 bar styles for every bar on the HUD (Kitsunebi, Flat, Smooth, Glossy, Glass, Bevel, Inset, Neon, Brushed, Striped, Scanline, Split, Fade, Outline, Minimal)
[/list]
[b]Move HUD[/b] lets you drag, resize or hide every HUD piece (stat bars, toolbelt, compass, location, trackers, chat, subtitles, ammo, looted items, vehicle bars, pop-ups, enemy health, party bars, the minimap, the crafting card, the unit frames, the party frames, the XP bar and the action bar) on a soft grid with a centre line, with optional snap to grid and a reset button. The stat bars can stay together, come apart in pairs (Health & Stamina, Food & Water) or each be a piece of its own. [b]Profiles[/b] save your whole layout by name, and you can export one as a line of text to share with friends. Presets (Minimal, Full, Streamer) switch HUD pieces on and off in one click. Saved per player, so on a server everyone picks their own; the defaults look the same as without it.

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]Languages[/color][/b]

KitsuneUI follows your game's language setting. Most of what you see is the game's own text, already translated; KitsuneUI's own text (the settings window, move mode, the SHIFT and TIP tags and the loading tips) is translated into all 13 languages the game supports: English, German, Spanish, French, Italian, Japanese, Korean, Polish, Brazilian Portuguese, Russian, Turkish, and Simplified and Traditional Chinese. The translations are ours, not native-reviewed, so corrections are welcome.

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]Install[/color][/b]

Unzip each zip you want into your [b]Mods[/b] folder, so you end up with Mods/KitsuneUI/ModInfo.xml. The Mods folder is in your game folder, or at %APPDATA%\7DaysToDie\Mods.

[b]Playing on a server:[/b] install it on the [b]server and on every player's game[/b]. The server sends its UI layout to everyone who joins, so a player without KitsuneUI sees blank spots where the theme's images go. Tell your players before you add it.

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]Compatibility[/color][/b]

Windows that other mods resize are restyled in place and keep their vanilla names. Their glass panels are anchored to the grids rather than sized with fixed numbers, so bigger bags, containers, toolbelts and workstation grids still fit.

Mods that rebuild the same windows from scratch (full UI overhauls) will clash with it. Use one UI theme at a time.

Built and tested on 7 Days to Die V3.3 and V3.4.

[b]For mod authors:[/b] your mod's windows can wear the Kitsunebi glass, follow each player's look settings and join Move HUD, and stay vanilla for players without KitsuneUI. See [url=https://github.com/Kitsune-Den/KitsuneUI/blob/main/docs/framework.md]Building on KitsuneUI[/url].

[b][color=rgb(217, 70, 239)]Removing it[/color][/b]

Delete the folders. The theme stores nothing in your save; the Settings add-on keeps one small file, %APPDATA%\7DaysToDie\KitsuneUI\settings.txt (plus a profiles folder next to it if you saved any). Removing the Toolbelt20 add-on is safe for items too: the extra toolbelt items go into your backpack, and anything that doesn't fit drops at your feet.
